<Layouts.app flash={@flash}>
  <header class="mb-6">
    <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-base-content/45">API</p>
    <h1 class="mt-1 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The GraphQL endpoint</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-[13px] leading-6 text-base-content/60">
      Veritas has one service layer, and three faces on it: this interface, the CLI, and the
      GraphQL API. A change made through any one of them is a change through all of them, and
      it lands in the same Audit Log.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="v-panel px-4 py-4">
    <h2 class="text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight">The endpoint answers POST, not GET</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-base-content/65">
      A GraphQL query is a body, not a path, so the endpoint is <span class="font-mono text-base-content">POST&nbsp;/graphql</span>. A browser that
      navigates to <span class="font-mono text-base-content">/graphql</span> with a GET lands
      on this page instead of on a query result.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-base-content/45">Query it</p>
        <pre class="mt-1 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-base-300 bg-base-100 p-3 font-mono text-[12px] leading-5"><code>curl -s http://127.0.0.1:4000/graphql \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  --data '&lbrace;"query":"&lbrace; __typename &rbrace;"&rbrace;'</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-base-content/45">Browse it</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-[13px] leading-6 text-base-content/65">
          The GraphiQL IDE is served at <a
            href="/graphql/i"
            class="text-brand underline underline-offset-2"
          >/graphql/i</a>.
          It runs against the same schema, against this same installation.
        </p>
        <a
          href="/graphql/i"
          class="mt-2 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[13px] text-brand underline underline-offset-2"
        >
          Open GraphiQL <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="v-panel mt-3 px-4 py-4">
    <h2 class="text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-tight">What is in the schema today</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-base-content/65">
      The schema is owned by the service layer, and it grows as the domain does. The
      Incidents, the Audit Log and the Configuration Items are the parts that exist now; the
      Changes, the Service Requests, the Problems and the Vulnerabilities are ticket types the
      domain already knows, and they join the schema as their screens arrive.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-base-content/65">
      The schema is the one source of truth for what a query can ask for, so
      <span class="font-mono text-base-content">/graphql/i</span>
      is always the authoritative
      answer to that question — the documentation can be a release behind it, and this cannot.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layouts.app>
